    "result_text": "Build succeeds with all tests green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily registration limit (429) by mirroring the existing per-city capacity rule, keeping each concern in its own small unit:\n\n**New function** \u2014 `RequireDailyRegistrationCapacity` (`function/owner/`): counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals today (`LocalDate.now()`) and throws `DailyRegistrationLimitException` once the count reaches `DAILY_LIMIT` (100). It only injects `OwnerRepository`, since the rule is a pure count independent of the request body.\n\n**New checked exception** \u2014 `DailyRegistrationLimitException` (`escalation/`): extends `Exception` so it surfaces in the `throws` clause.\n\n**New handler** \u2014 `DailyRegistrationLimitExceptionHandler` (`escalation/`): responds `429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` with a `ProblemDetail`, reusing the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper exactly like the other handlers.\n\n**Wiring**:\n- `officefloor/escalation/...DailyRegistrationLimitException.yml` \u2192 maps the exception to the handler.\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2192 inserted a `requireDailyCapacity` step (under the same `transaction` governance) right after `requireCityCapacity` and before `build`, so the request is rejected before any owner is constructed or saved.\n\nThe `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` test (create 100 owners dated today, expect the 101st to be `429`) passes, and the full suite is green.",
